text:
The first lines, "Oh! for thy gentle voice again,"
are echoed by these words from your own song :—

Now have we come to where our souls may meet;
Here is a love-feast, and no place for fear ;

Be careful! Careful now you are;
 A fly is crawling on your hair.
 Look out! It's going up your nose;
 Look sharp and get it off before it goes
 Right down your throat, where nothing can save it.

When the king was sick, we saw his pride go down;

We heard him coughing at the courtly round;

And through the city came a sigh and groan:

For death is never quite the thing to own.

Now let us hear what they will say when he

Is dead! Then all our fears are satisfied —

O'er every field, where living men do dwell;

Where each, in vain, pursues his idle wish ;

While he alone, that seeks himself to know,

Can find the truth which makes him wise and free.

The hour is come, and now I die ;

 God’s holy laws have left me here :

 O Christ my Saviour! hear me cry ;

 Now spare me from that death to fear !

 Thy precious blood has bought for me

 The highest joys that heaven can give,

 And made my soul its dwelling free;

 But oh, Lord ! do not let it live !
